12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Classify the three kinematic modes of planar rigid body motion: pure translation, fixed-axis rotation, and general plane motion. State the velocity and acceleration relationships governing each mode.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Classification of Planar Rigid Body Kinematics:) Tj
T*
(   A rigid body is defined as an ideal continuum of matter where the distance between any two) Tj
T*
(   constituent particles remains strictly invariant under applied loads.) Tj
T*
(   In 2D planar motion, every particle moves in parallel planes. The motion is classified into:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mode 1: Pure Translation \(Rectilinear or Curvilinear\):) Tj
T*
(   - Definition: Every line segment in the body remains strictly parallel to its initial) Tj
T*
(   orientation.) Tj
T*
(   - Velocity Field: All points in the body possess identical velocity vectors at every instant:) Tj
T*
(   v_A\(t\) = v_B\(t\) = v_G\(t\)) Tj
T*
(   - Acceleration Field: All points possess identical linear acceleration vectors:) Tj
T*
(   a_A\(t\) = a_B\(t\) = a_G\(t\)) Tj
T*
(   - Angular Velocity & Acceleration: omega = 0 and alpha = 0.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Mode 2: Fixed-Axis Rotation:) Tj
T*
(   - Definition: All particles move in concentric circular paths about a permanent stationary) Tj
T*
(   axis.) Tj
T*
(   - Kinematic Relations for particle at radius r from axis:) Tj
T*
(   Linear velocity: v = r * omega \(directed tangentially\)) Tj
T*
(   Tangential acceleration: a_t = r * alpha \(rate of change of speed\)) Tj
T*
(   Normal acceleration: a_n = r * omega^2 = v^2 / r \(directed inward to axis\)) Tj
T*
(   Total acceleration magnitude: a = sqrt\( a_t^2 + a_n^2 \) = r * sqrt\( alpha^2 + omega^4 \)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Mode 3: General Plane Motion \(Simultaneous Translation and Rotation\):) Tj
T*
(   - Definition: A planar motion that is neither a pure translation nor a pure rotation.) Tj
T*
(   - Relative Velocity Formulation: Choose an arbitrary reference base point A:) Tj
T*
(   v_B = v_A + v_B/A = v_A + \(omega x r_B/A\)) Tj
T*
(   Magnitude: |v_B/A| = omega * r_B/A, directed perpendicular to line AB.) Tj
T*
(   - Relative Acceleration Formulation:) Tj
T*
(   a_B = a_A + a_B/A_t + a_B/A_n = a_A + \(alpha x r_B/A\) - omega^2 * r_B/A) Tj
T*
(   where \(a_B/A\)_t = alpha * r_B/A \(tangential\) and \(a_B/A\)_n = omega^2 * r_B/A \(centripetal) Tj
T*
(   inward to A\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Define the Instantaneous Centre of Zero Velocity \(ICR\). Explain the geometric methods used to locate the ICR for general planar motion and for rolling without slipping.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition and Fundamental Theorem of ICR:) Tj
T*
(   The Instantaneous Centre of Zero Velocity \(ICR or ICZ\) is a unique point in the plane of motion) Tj
T*
(   \(which may lie on or off the physical boundary of the rigid body\) that has ZERO instantaneous) Tj
T*
(   linear velocity at that particular instant of time: v_ICR = 0.) Tj
T*
(   Chasles's Theorem establishes that any general planar motion of a rigid body at a given instant) Tj
T*
(   is kinematically equivalent to a pure rotation about its instantaneous center ICR.) Tj
T*
(   Consequently, the velocity of ANY point P on the body is given by:) Tj
T*
(   v_P = omega * r_P/ICR) Tj
T*
(   directed perpendicular to the ray connecting ICR to point P in the sense of angular velocity) Tj
T*
(   omega.) Tj
T*
ET

