T*
(   2. Geometric Methods to Locate the ICR:) Tj
T*
(   Method A [Non-Parallel Velocity Directions at Two Points]:) Tj
T*
(   - Suppose velocities v_A and v_B at two distinct points A and B are known in direction.) Tj
T*
(   - Since v_A must be perpendicular to radius line A-ICR, draw a straight line perpendicular to) Tj
T*
(   v_A at A.) Tj
T*
(   - Draw another straight line perpendicular to v_B at B.) Tj
T*
(   - The geometric intersection point of these two perpendicular lines uniquely defines the ICR!) Tj
T*
(   Method B [Parallel Velocities Perpendicular to Line AB]:) Tj
T*
(   - If v_A and v_B are parallel and perpendicular to line AB:) Tj
T*
(   * Same direction \(v_A != v_B\): Construct similar velocity triangles; ICR lies along line AB) Tj
T*
(   extended.) Tj
T*
(   * Opposite directions: Connect vector arrowheads; ICR lies between A and B on line AB.) Tj
T*
(   * Same direction and magnitude \(v_A = v_B\): ICR is at INFINITY \(pure translation, omega = 0\).) Tj
T*
(   Method C [Rolling Without Slipping]:) Tj
T*
(   - When a body rolls without slipping along a stationary curved or flat surface, the point of) Tj
T*
(   instantaneous contact is completely at rest relative to the surface.) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore, the instantaneous contact point IS THE ICR.) Tj
