(   [Problem Statement]: A flywheel of mass m = 400 kg and radius of gyration k_G = 0.60 m is rotating at N1 = 720 RPM. A mechanical friction brake applies a constant retarding torque of T_brake = 350 N-m. Determine: \(1\) Time required to bring the flywheel to rest, \(2\) Number of revolutions completed before stopping, \(3\) Total heat energy dissipated in the brake.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Initial Kinematic Properties & Mass Moment of Inertia:) Tj
T*
(   Mass: m = 400 kg; Radius of gyration: k_G = 0.60 m.) Tj
T*
(   Mass moment of inertia: I_G = m * k_G^2 = 400 * \(0.60\)^2 = 400 * 0.36 = 144.0 kg-m^2.) Tj
T*
(   Initial angular velocity omega1:) Tj
T*
(   omega1 = 2 * pi * N1 / 60 = 2 * pi * 720 / 60 = 24 * pi = 75.398 rad/s.) Tj
T*
(   Final angular velocity: omega2 = 0 rad/s.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Angular Deceleration and Stopping Time \(t_stop\):) Tj
T*
(   Applying rotational equation of motion \(Sigma M = I_G * alpha\):) Tj
T*
(   - T_brake = I_G * alpha) Tj
T*
(   - 350 N-m = 144.0 * alpha => alpha = - 350 / 144.0 = - 2.4306 rad/s^2.) Tj
T*
(   Using constant angular acceleration relation: omega2 = omega1 + alpha * t:) Tj
T*
(   0 = 75.398 - 2.4306 * t_stop) Tj
T*
(   t_stop = 75.398 / 2.4306 = 31.02 seconds.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Total Angular Displacement and Revolutions \(theta, n_rev\):) Tj
T*
(   Using omega2^2 = omega1^2 + 2 * alpha * theta:) Tj
T*
(   0 = \(75.398\)^2 + 2 * \(- 2.4306\) * theta) Tj
T*
(   4.8612 * theta = 5684.86) Tj
T*
(   theta = 5684.86 / 4.8612 = 1169.44 radians.) Tj
T*
(   Number of revolutions:) Tj
T*
(   n_rev = theta / \(2 * pi\) = 1169.44 / \(2 * 3.14159\) = 186.12 revolutions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Total Energy Dissipated in Friction Brake:) Tj
T*
(   By the Work-Energy Theorem, heat dissipated equals initial kinetic energy:) Tj
T*
(   KE_initial = 0.5 * I_G * omega1^2) Tj
T*
(   KE_initial = 0.5 * 144.0 * \(75.398\)^2 = 72.0 * 5684.86 = 409,310 J = 409.31 kJ.) Tj
T*
(   Verification via Work: U_brake = T_brake * theta = 350 N-m * 1169.44 rad = 409,304 J = 409.3 kJ) Tj
T*
(   \(Exact match!\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 9:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A civil crane hoisting drum of mass M = 150 kg and outer radius R = 0.40 m \(radius of gyration k_G = 0.30 m\) is used to lower a structural steel beam of mass m = 250 kg. If the drum is unbraked and rotates freely without friction, determine: \(1\) Downward acceleration of the beam, \(2\) Tension in the hoisting cable.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Moment of Inertia of Drum & Free Body Diagrams:) Tj
T*
(   Drum properties: M = 150 kg, k_G = 0.30 m, outer radius R = 0.40 m.) Tj
T*
(   Mass moment of inertia of drum: I_O = M * k_G^2 = 150 * \(0.30\)^2 = 150 * 0.09 = 13.50 kg-m^2.) Tj
T*
(   Suspended beam: Mass m = 250 kg; Weight W = m * g = 250 * 9.81 = 2452.5 N.) Tj
T*
(   Let 'a' be the downward linear acceleration of the beam, and T be the cable tension.) Tj
T*
(   Kinematic constraint: Drum rotates with angular acceleration alpha = a / R.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
endobj
